description:
Fork-side change, same fork branch and release as the odometer/journal leaf (design section 3.3 and 6).

What it builds:
- every history snapshot read the port takes (the HISTORY point-tag path behind Terminal.HistoryRows) answers one atomic snapshot: rows, total, retained floor (the odometer value below which nothing is retained), and a layout generation;
- the generation advances on every layout-invalidating reflow and every destructive retention change; prune and ED3 advance floor AND generation, never the odometer;
- no three-read derivation: floor/head/generation are one read, so the Go port can stop deriving the floor as screenDepartedRows - HistoryRows.Total.

DONE WHEN: fork-side tests show (1) a width reflow preserving every oldest tagged fragment in HISTORY changes generation but neither odometer nor the exact ordinal floor; a fragment moved into the active area no longer counts as retained history, (2) reflow followed by real partial page pruning moves the floor to the last wholly unretained departure ordinal, changes generation and leaves odometer unchanged, (3) ED3 sets floor to odometer and advances generation without a departure (floor strictly increases only when tagged history existed before ED3), and (4) scalar rows, total, floor, generation and odometer come from one C snapshot call under documented caller serialization. Test a Go history read interleaved with Ingest/Resize/ED3 under the existing terminal mutex in the later port leaf nocx-nv3in, not by racing calls on the non-reentrant C handle. Charge surviving allocated provenance capacity alongside PageList pages to the existing heuristic live-history retention byte limit; report transient old+new resize peak separately, since existing PageList reflow may exceed the limit temporarily. On metadata OOM, do not publish a floor inconsistent with retained content; retention may drop old live history but never the separately captured departure journal.

Helper-side accounting in internal/helper/session (design .internal/specs/2026-10-04-history-erased-callback-design.md, section 5.7 and 9). REOPENED 2026-10-04: like nocx-ho1ri, this was wrongly closed as journal-superseded; the byte-pool accounting is needed for the reopened retained-window resend, not for the fork journal.

What it builds:
- ONE byte-credit pool under the session's existing history.helperBufferMB, charged by the helper's retained delivered rows (the resend window), the pending wire FIFO, closing screens and markers - no new setting, no hidden budget, ADR-0075 unamended;
- ownership counting: each byte spends once per simultaneous owner; a copy across the C/Go boundary or between the retained window and the FIFO briefly holds two, and BOTH count - the transient double is charged, with a test;
- overflow takes ADR-0075's existing path: the block in flight ends incomplete from the named row, rows drop until the next end marker (carried with its own fence), recording resumes at the next command; MinRowBufferBytes still guarantees the marker fits;
- live-retention loss and helper-buffer overflow are distinguishable in every emission.

DONE WHEN: helper tests show (a) a transient double-copy stays inside the budget, (b) pool exhaustion names the row and ends the block incomplete per ADR-0075 while the next command records again, and (c) the two loss causes produce distinguishable emissions with their own words.

Fork-side change on the shady2k/ghostty pin branch (design: .internal/specs/2026-10-02-departure-journal-design.md sections 3 and 4).

What it builds, behind the libghostty-vt C ABI:
- a monotonic departure odometer for the primary screen, advancing once per row crossing the top of the primary active area, never advanced by prune, ED3, reset or reflow;
- a pull journal staged at BOTH crossing paths — the grow() history append and the zero-retention in-place shift (no_scrollback/cursorDownScroll/eraseRow) — capturing cells, style and wrap flags at the instant of crossing, with the odometer span it covers; bounded staging charged to the shared pool (the Go side of the pool is a later leaf);
- a drain entry point returning rows oldest-first; fully drained after every Ingest and every Resize return;
- a shrink while the alternate screen holds the pane emits hidden primary rows into the journal at Resize, once, and not again on return; alt-screen scrolling emits nothing;
- marker/block attribution stays in Go: the journal carries rows and odometer arithmetic, no nonce, no interval state (ADR-0024 decision 1).

DONE WHEN: fork-side tests show (a) at zero retention every scrolled row is journaled, (b) a same-feed prune advances the retained floor and never the odometer, and never conflates its own departures with pruned pages, (c) hidden-primary shrink emits exactly once, and (d) alt-screen output emits nothing — the shapes internal/sessionruntime/alt_screen_resize_rows_test.go already pins, at the fork's own seam.

Contract work: contracts/, the helper DTO, sessionruntime's HistoryPageView and the transport conformance tests (design section 6). Depends on the port leaf.

What it builds:
- a layout generation on HistoryPageView, the helper DTO, the session.historyPage wire result and the correlated rows carrier; the schema's start description is corrected — a layout-invalidating reflow moves live positions, and the generation says when;
- a live-history cursor is (generation, before): a stale generation is answered explicitly (stale/reset naming the current generation and floor), never rows measured against a layout the caller never saw;
- floor, head and generation come from the fork's atomic snapshot; the arithmetic floor derivation is gone with the port leaf.

DONE WHEN: contracts:check passes, the DTO-conforms test passes, and the OVER-THE-WIRE conformance test reads a generation off the real socket. A real prune and ED3 set the exact ordinal floor and advance generation (ED3 floor=odometer, even if no tagged history existed); a stale cursor gets an explicit reset, never a short list. A port and over-the-wire paging test starts with O=0, retained_floor=0 and two untagged physical history rows created by active-only width reflow: both rows remain pageable under the new generation without treating the ordinal floor as the physical page count. Distinguish generation-local physical cursor coordinates from the ordinal retention watermark.

Runtime work in internal/sessionruntime (design section 5). Depends on the port leaf.

What it builds:
- the marker-split ordering is preserved and now journal-fed: sub-chunk to the marker, drain that sub-chunk's journal entries, closing-screen suppression, stream the kept logical rows, THEN snapshot departedRows, then the fence sighting rebases the next interval, then bytes after the marker are fed;
- EndRow stays LOGICAL departedRows (post-suppression) everywhere — splitObservationAtFenceLocked and the completion-first seal keep their meaning; raw journal Next only delimits drains and is never an interval boundary;
- screenDepartedRows keeps its role for the closing-screen cut arithmetic; the journal's odometer is its raw counterpart.

DONE WHEN: runtime tests show raw-vs-logical divergence at a marker (a suppressed closing screen's rows counted raw but not logical, EndRow logical), and marker+following-bytes feeds keep rows before the end marker in the right block with the next interval rebased after it — including the error and close paths draining the journal.

Go port work in internal/emulator/ghostty (design sections 3, 4, 5). Depends on the re-pin.

What it builds:
- DepartedRows is fed by the journal drain (per sub-feed, oldest-first, odometer span), replacing the conflated depth-count report; the retention paragraph of its contract loses the same-feed-prune hole and gains the journal's guarantee;
- the odometer, retained floor and layout generation are read through the ABI as one snapshot; the port stops deriving the floor arithmetically;
- the C journal's staged peak is reported to/bounded by the session's byte-credit pool seam so the helper-side leaf can charge it under history.helperBufferMB (the pool's Go accounting is a later leaf; this leaf exposes the bound and the peak);
- ApplyScrollback's re-baseline semantics survive: a prune advances floor and generation, never the odometer, so the runtime's re-baseline borrow keeps its meaning.

DONE WHEN: port tests at real geometry show capture complete at DEFAULT retention, at 100k (where the 32 MiB ceiling binds) and at ZERO retention (live discard immediate, capture complete), a same-feed prune is no longer a conflated error, and a closed terminal releases the journal with no leak (drain/release on success, error and close). A deterministic port-level test interleaves HistoryRows with concurrent Ingest/Resize/ED3 under the existing terminal mutex: each answer has rows, total, floor and generation from one before-or-after layout, never a mixture; the second read sees the changed generation. The fork C handle is non-reentrant, so this tests the supported Go seam rather than racing direct C calls.

