description:
Fork-side implementation of the owner-approved callback (nocx-zg3k3.5.18, design
.internal/specs/2026-10-04-history-erased-callback-design.md, sections 3-4).

What it builds, behind the libghostty-vt C ABI:
- one new effect in the existing effects table (GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_OPT_*, include/ghostty/vt/terminal.h), same synchronous callback mechanism as BELL, RESET, SEMANTIC_PROMPT: "a history page is about to be erased", carrying a sized GhosttyTerminalHistoryErased {size, first_row, count} span in HISTORY coordinates;
- PageList.zig fires it at exactly four sites, verified by reading at befcdfd2: Limits.enforce right before erasePage(first) (L7112); grow's byte-limit prune block before popFirst (L4045); eraseHistory/eraseRows before each full-page erasePage (L5580) and before the partial boundary-page shift/resetRow (L5596-5635); reset before releasePages (L1012). It does NOT fire in reflow's destroyNode, compact/split/increaseCapacity, compression, or trimTrailingBlankRows (content survives there, or it is active-area blank trim);
- plumbing: PageList gains an optional history_erased callback field set from Screen/Terminal init options, null default (byte-for-byte unchanged build for callers that never set it);
- fork-side Zig tests: each fire site fires once, with the exact span, in destruction order, before content dies; reflow does not fire; a null callback changes nothing.

DONE WHEN: fork tests show (1) a prune via enforce fires [0, first.rows()) before the page is erased, (2) grow's byte-limit reuse fires [0, rows) before the memory is reused, (3) ED3 erases all history with spans covering every destroyed row (full pages and boundary page), (4) RIS fires over the retained history span before releasePages, (5) reflow resizes fire nothing, and (6) the C ABI option is installable/clearable by NULL like every other effect. Release: six manifest targets rebuilt from the new fork commit, MANIFEST.json hashes updated, fetch-verify and make vt-verify-link green, published as a fork release tag (nocx-v8d69's mechanics). No nocx-side code in this leaf.

Go-port implementation of the owner-approved callback (design .internal/specs/2026-10-04-history-erased-callback-design.md, sections 5-6, 9-10). Depends on the fork effect leaf (nocx-zg3k3.5.19) and the re-pinned archive.

What it builds:
- bridge.c installs the new effect in nocxInstall (one option, GhosttyTerminalHistoryErasedFn), and effects.go gains the handler: it copies rows [first_row, first_row+count) through the existing HISTORY-tag read path while the callback runs (reads inside effects are already established; no vt_write reentrancy) into the existing bounded departed-row stream;
- dedupe: rows of a span that were already captured at an earlier write boundary are skipped by the port's captured-frontier cursor; rows above it are appended. No odometer is read from the fork; the frontier is the port's own count;
- the REP counterexample (11 bytes `x ESC[1000000b`) at width 80 and width 1, ED3/RIS same-write, near-full prune, and resize/reflow all reproduce the design's §5 checks at port level.

DONE WHEN: port tests at real geometry show, with the real linked archive: complete capture at DEFAULT retention, at a near-full budget, at ZERO retention (via the other leaf's floor), for the 11-byte REP counterexample (>=12,476 departures at width 80, >=999,977 at width 1, all captured), for ED3 and RIS inside one write, and for shrink/grow resizes — with no duplicates and no gaps; the departed stream stays in the library's destruction order; a closed terminal drains the stream and releases with no leak; the effect handler never blocks on a second lock (runs under the terminal mutex like every other effect).

Nocx-side zero-live-history half of the design (.internal/specs/2026-10-04-history-erased-callback-design.md, section 7). Depends on the port leaf (nocx-zg3k3.5.20).

What it builds:
- internal/emulator/ghostty ApplyScrollback: the zero branch keeps a NONZERO byte budget (the existing 32 MiB ceiling) and applies lines=0, which the library reads as its own page floor (measured ~399 rows at 80 cols), so rows always pass through pages and the history-erased effect captures them. The destructive bytes=0 mapping is gone;
- the live scrollback surface honours scrollbackLines=0 nocx-side: the helper history-page op / sessionruntime ReadHistoryPage serve no rows at the setting 0, so the person still sees "no live scrollback";
- TestApplyScrollbackZeroLeavesNoHistory is rewritten to the new contract (retained content = bounded floor, durable stream complete, live surface empty).

DONE WHEN: a session at scrollbackLines=0 completes a transcript and the restored card holds every row after restart while the live history page op serves none; the port test above is green; the settings screen text still says the live value erases live scrollback, which it does.
